This `EmailThread` can never extend a base service class, and a pool will reject it.

```java
// Couples the task to a thread AND uses up single inheritance
class EmailThread extends Thread {
    private final String recipient;

    EmailThread(String recipient) {
        this.recipient = recipient;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Sending email to " + recipient);
    }
}

// new EmailThread("a@b.com").start();   // works, but...
// executor.execute(new EmailThread(...)); // pointless: it's run as a plain Runnable, the Thread features are wasted
```

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

// The task is just a task - inheritance stays free, reuse is easy
class EmailTask implements Runnable {
    private final String recipient;

    EmailTask(String recipient) {
        this.recipient = recipient;
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Sending email to " + recipient);
    }
}

public class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(5);
        executor.execute(new EmailTask("user@example.com")); // pool runs it
        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```
*The same `EmailTask` also works with `new Thread(task).start()`—define once, run anywhere.*

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

// Runnable - no return value, catches exceptions internally
class NotificationTask implements Runnable {
    private final String userId;
    NotificationTask(String userId) { this.userId = userId; }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Notification sent to " + userId);
    }
}

// Callable - returns a result, can throw checked exceptions
class UserFetchTask implements Callable<String> {
    private final long userId;
    UserFetchTask(long userId) { this.userId = userId; }

    @Override
    public String call() throws Exception {   // checked exceptions allowed
        return "User-" + userId;              // returns the result
    }
}

public class Demo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        var executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);
        executor.execute(new NotificationTask("user123"));   // fire and forget

        Future<String> future = executor.submit(new UserFetchTask(100L));
        String name = future.get();   // blocks until the result is ready
        System.out.println("Fetched: " + name);
        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```

The loop counter isn't effectively final, so this fails to compile.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

var executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);
for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
    // Compile error: variable used in lambda must be final or effectively final
    executor.execute(() -> System.out.println("Task " + i));
}
```

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class RunnableLambdaDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(3);

        // Lambda - the concise way to make a Runnable
        executor.execute(() -> System.out.println("Lambda task"));

        // Method reference - maps an existing no-arg void method
        executor.execute(RunnableLambdaDemo::logStatus);

        for (int i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
            final int taskId = i;        // copy into a final local first
            executor.execute(() -> System.out.println("Task " + taskId));
        }
        executor.shutdown();
    }

    static void logStatus() {            // no params, void return - a valid Runnable
        System.out.println("Status check");
    }
}
```

If the task throws, `execute` swallows it—no stack trace, no signal.

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

var executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);
Runnable risky = () -> { throw new RuntimeException("boom"); };
executor.execute(risky);   // exception is silently lost
```

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class CompatibilityDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) throws Exception {
        Runnable task = () ->
            System.out.println("Running on " + Thread.currentThread().getName());

        // 1) Raw thread - one-off, no pool
        Thread t = new Thread(task);
        t.start();
        t.join();

        // 2) Pool: execute (fire-and-forget) and submit (returns Future)
        var executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);
        executor.execute(task);
        Future<?> future = executor.submit(task);
        future.get();   // blocks; surfaces any exception the task threw
        System.out.println("Done: " + future.isDone());
        executor.shutdown();
    }
}
```
*The same `task` ran three ways—no change to its code.*

This wastes the inheritance slot and can't be pooled—the task should be a `Runnable`.

```java
// Anti-pattern: no thread behaviour is customised, it's just a task
class ReportThread extends Thread {
    @Override
    public void run() {
        System.out.println("Generating report...");
    }
}
// executor.submit(new ReportThread()); // treated as a plain Runnable - Thread features wasted
```

```java
// Valid use: the subclass instruments the thread's own run lifecycle
class MonitoredThread extends Thread {
    private long elapsedMs;

    MonitoredThread(Runnable task, String name) {
        super(task, name);
    }

    @Override
    public void run() {
        long start = System.currentTimeMillis();
        try {
            super.run();                       // runs the wrapped Runnable
        } finally {
            elapsedMs = System.currentTimeMillis() - start;
            System.out.println(getName() + " took " + elapsedMs + "ms");
        }
    }

    long getElapsedMs() { return elapsedMs; } // method beyond the standard API
}
// Note: a MonitoredThread still cannot be submitted to an ExecutorService.
```

On a reused pool thread, the next request inherits the previous user's context.

```java
public class UserContext {
    private static final ThreadLocal<String> CURRENT = new ThreadLocal<>();

    static void handle(String userId) {
        CURRENT.set(userId);
        System.out.println("Handling for " + CURRENT.get());
        // BUG: never cleared - the next task on this thread sees the old userId
    }
}
```

```java
import java.util.concurrent.*;

public class UserContext {
    private static final ThreadLocal<String> CURRENT = new ThreadLocal<>();

    static void handle(String userId) {
        CURRENT.set(userId);
        try {
            process();                 // CURRENT.get() available anywhere on this thread
        } finally {
            CURRENT.remove();          // critical: clear before the thread is reused
        }
    }

    static void process() {
        System.out.println("Processing for " + CURRENT.get());
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        var pool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(2);
        pool.execute(() -> handle("user-1"));
        pool.execute(() -> handle("user-2"));
        pool.shutdown();
    }
}
```
